.SS "\fBGLFWAPI\fP \fBGLFWcursor\fP * glfwCreateCursor (const \fBGLFWimage\fP * image, int xhot, int yhot)"

.PP
Creates a custom cursor\&. Creates a new custom cursor image that can be set for a window with \fBglfwSetCursor\fP\&. The cursor can be destroyed with \fBglfwDestroyCursor\fP\&. Any remaining cursors are destroyed by \fBglfwTerminate\fP\&.
.PP
The pixels are 32-bit, little-endian, non-premultiplied RGBA, i\&.e\&. eight bits per channel with the red channel first\&. They are arranged canonically as packed sequential rows, starting from the top-left corner\&.
.PP
The cursor hotspot is specified in pixels, relative to the upper-left corner of the cursor image\&. Like all other coordinate systems in GLFW, the X-axis points to the right and the Y-axis points down\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIimage\fP The desired cursor image\&. 
.br
\fIxhot\fP The desired x-coordinate, in pixels, of the cursor hotspot\&. 
.br
\fIyhot\fP The desired y-coordinate, in pixels, of the cursor hotspot\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The handle of the created cursor, or \fRNULL\fP if an \fBerror\fP occurred\&.
.RE
.PP
@errors Possible errors include \fBGLFW_NOT_INITIALIZED\fP, \fBGLFW_INVALID_VALUE\fP and \fBGLFW_PLATFORM_ERROR\fP\&.
.PP
@pointer_lifetime The specified image data is copied before this function returns\&.
.PP
@thread_safety This function must only be called from the main thread\&.
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBcursor_object\fP 
.PP
\fBglfwDestroyCursor\fP 
.PP
\fBglfwCreateStandardCursor\fP
.RE
.PP
\fBSince\fP
.RS 4
Added in version 3\&.1\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "\fBGLFWAPI\fP \fBGLFWcursor\fP * glfwCreateStandardCursor (int shape)"

.PP
Creates a cursor with a standard shape\&. Returns a cursor with a standard shape, that can be set for a window with \fBglfwSetCursor\fP\&. The images for these cursors come from the system cursor theme and their exact appearance will vary between platforms\&.
.PP
Most of these shapes are guaranteed to exist on every supported platform but a few may not be present\&. See the table below for details\&.
.PP
Cursor shape   Windows   macOS   X11   Wayland    \fBGLFW_ARROW_CURSOR\fP   Yes   Yes   Yes   Yes    \fBGLFW_IBEAM_CURSOR\fP   Yes   Yes   Yes   Yes    \fBGLFW_CROSSHAIR_CURSOR\fP   Yes   Yes   Yes   Yes    \fBGLFW_POINTING_HAND_CURSOR\fP   Yes   Yes   Yes   Yes    \fBGLFW_RESIZE_EW_CURSOR\fP   Yes   Yes   Yes   Yes    \fBGLFW_RESIZE_NS_CURSOR\fP   Yes   Yes   Yes   Yes    \fBGLFW_RESIZE_NWSE_CURSOR\fP   Yes   Yes\*{1\*}    Maybe\*{2\*}    Maybe\*{2\*}     \fBGLFW_RESIZE_NESW_CURSOR\fP   Yes   Yes\*{1\*}    Maybe\*{2\*}    Maybe\*{2\*}     \fBGLFW_RESIZE_ALL_CURSOR\fP   Yes   Yes   Yes   Yes    \fBGLFW_NOT_ALLOWED_CURSOR\fP   Yes   Yes   Maybe\*{2\*}    Maybe\*{2\*}    
.PP
1) This uses a private system API and may fail in the future\&.
.PP
2) This uses a newer standard that not all cursor themes support\&.
.PP
If the requested shape is not available, this function emits a \fBGLFW_CURSOR_UNAVAILABLE\fP error and returns \fRNULL\fP\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIshape\fP One of the \fBstandard shapes\fP\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
A new cursor ready to use or \fRNULL\fP if an \fBerror\fP occurred\&.
.RE
.PP
@errors Possible errors include \fBGLFW_NOT_INITIALIZED\fP, \fBGLFW_INVALID_ENUM\fP, \fBGLFW_CURSOR_UNAVAILABLE\fP and \fBGLFW_PLATFORM_ERROR\fP\&.
.PP
@thread_safety This function must only be called from the main thread\&.
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBcursor_standard\fP 
.PP
\fBglfwCreateCursor\fP
.RE
.PP
\fBSince\fP
.RS 4
Added in version 3\&.1\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "\fBGLFWAPI\fP \fBvoid\fP glfwDestroyCursor (\fBGLFWcursor\fP * cursor)"

.PP
Destroys a cursor\&. This function destroys a cursor previously created with \fBglfwCreateCursor\fP\&. Any remaining cursors will be destroyed by \fBglfwTerminate\fP\&.
.PP
If the specified cursor is current for any window, that window will be reverted to the default cursor\&. This does not affect the cursor mode\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIcursor\fP The cursor object to destroy\&.
.RE
.PP
@errors Possible errors include \fBGLFW_NOT_INITIALIZED\fP and \fBGLFW_PLATFORM_ERROR\fP\&.
.PP
@reentrancy This function must not be called from a callback\&.
.PP
@thread_safety This function must only be called from the main thread\&.
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBcursor_object\fP 
.PP
\fBglfwCreateCursor\fP
.RE
.PP
\fBSince\fP
.RS 4
Added in version 3\&.1\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "\fBGLFWAPI\fP const char * glfwGetClipboardString (\fBGLFWwindow\fP * window)"

.PP
Returns the contents of the clipboard as a string\&. This function returns the contents of the system clipboard, if it contains or is convertible to a UTF-8 encoded string\&. If the clipboard is empty or if its contents cannot be converted, \fRNULL\fP is returned and a \fBGLFW_FORMAT_UNAVAILABLE\fP error is generated\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIwindow\fP Deprecated\&. Any valid window or \fRNULL\fP\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The contents of the clipboard as a UTF-8 encoded string, or \fRNULL\fP if an \fBerror\fP occurred\&.
.RE
.PP
@errors Possible errors include \fBGLFW_NOT_INITIALIZED\fP, \fBGLFW_FORMAT_UNAVAILABLE\fP and \fBGLFW_PLATFORM_ERROR\fP\&.
.PP
\fBRemarks\fP
.RS 4
@win32 The clipboard on Windows has a single global lock for reading and writing\&. GLFW tries to acquire it a few times, which is almost always enough\&. If it cannot acquire the lock then this function emits \fBGLFW_PLATFORM_ERROR\fP and returns\&. It is safe to try this multiple times\&.
.RE
.PP
@pointer_lifetime The returned string is allocated and freed by GLFW\&. You should not free it yourself\&. It is valid until the next call to \fBglfwGetClipboardString\fP or \fBglfwSetClipboardString\fP, or until the library is terminated\&.
.PP
@thread_safety This function must only be called from the main thread\&.
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBclipboard\fP 
.PP
\fBglfwSetClipboardString\fP
.RE
.PP
\fBSince\fP
.RS 4
Added in version 3\&.0\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "\fBGLFWAPI\fP \fBvoid\fP glfwGetCursorPos (\fBGLFWwindow\fP * window, double * xpos, double * ypos)"

.PP
Retrieves the position of the cursor relative to the content area of the window\&. This function returns the position of the cursor, in screen coordinates, relative to the upper-left corner of the content area of the specified window\&.
.PP
If the cursor is disabled (with \fRGLFW_CURSOR_DISABLED\fP) then the cursor position is unbounded and limited only by the minimum and maximum values of a \fRdouble\fP\&.
.PP
The coordinate can be converted to their integer equivalents with the \fRfloor\fP function\&. Casting directly to an integer type works for positive coordinates, but fails for negative ones\&.
.PP
Any or all of the position arguments may be \fRNULL\fP\&. If an error occurs, all non-\fRNULL\fP position arguments will be set to zero\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIwindow\fP The desired window\&. 
.br
\fIxpos\fP Where to store the cursor x-coordinate, relative to the left edge of the content area, or \fRNULL\fP\&. 
.br
\fIypos\fP Where to store the cursor y-coordinate, relative to the to top edge of the content area, or \fRNULL\fP\&.
.RE
.PP
@errors Possible errors include \fBGLFW_NOT_INITIALIZED\fP and \fBGLFW_PLATFORM_ERROR\fP\&.
.PP
@thread_safety This function must only be called from the main thread\&.
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBcursor_pos\fP 
.PP
\fBglfwSetCursorPos\fP
.RE
.PP
\fBSince\fP
.RS 4
Added in version 3\&.0\&. Replaces \fRglfwGetMousePos\fP\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "\fBGLFWAPI\fP const char * glfwGetGamepadName (int jid)"

.PP
Returns the human-readable gamepad name for the specified joystick\&. This function returns the human-readable name of the gamepad from the gamepad mapping assigned to the specified joystick\&.
.PP
If the specified joystick is not present or does not have a gamepad mapping this function will return \fRNULL\fP but will not generate an error\&. Call \fBglfwJoystickPresent\fP to check whether it is present regardless of whether it has a mapping\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIjid\fP The \fBjoystick\fP to query\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The UTF-8 encoded name of the gamepad, or \fRNULL\fP if the joystick is not present, does not have a mapping or an \fBerror\fP occurred\&.
.RE
.PP
@errors Possible errors include \fBGLFW_NOT_INITIALIZED\fP and \fBGLFW_INVALID_ENUM\fP\&.
.PP
@pointer_lifetime The returned string is allocated and freed by GLFW\&. You should not free it yourself\&. It is valid until the specified joystick is disconnected, the gamepad mappings are updated or the library is terminated\&.
.PP
@thread_safety This function must only be called from the main thread\&.
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBgamepad\fP 
.PP
\fBglfwJoystickIsGamepad\fP
.RE
.PP
\fBSince\fP
.RS 4
Added in version 3\&.3\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "\fBGLFWAPI\fP int glfwGetGamepadState (int jid, \fBGLFWgamepadstate\fP * state)"

.PP
Retrieves the state of the specified joystick remapped as a gamepad\&. This function retrieves the state of the specified joystick remapped to an Xbox-like gamepad\&.
.PP
If the specified joystick is not present or does not have a gamepad mapping this function will return \fRGLFW_FALSE\fP but will not generate an error\&. Call \fBglfwJoystickPresent\fP to check whether it is present regardless of whether it has a mapping\&.
.PP
The Guide button may not be available for input as it is often hooked by the system or the Steam client\&.
.PP
Not all devices have all the buttons or axes provided by \fBGLFWgamepadstate\fP\&. Unavailable buttons and axes will always report \fRGLFW_RELEASE\fP and 0\&.0 respectively\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIjid\fP The \fBjoystick\fP to query\&. 
.br
\fIstate\fP The gamepad input state of the joystick\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
\fRGLFW_TRUE\fP if successful, or \fRGLFW_FALSE\fP if no joystick is connected, it has no gamepad mapping or an \fBerror\fP occurred\&.
.RE
.PP
@errors Possible errors include \fBGLFW_NOT_INITIALIZED\fP and \fBGLFW_INVALID_ENUM\fP\&.
.PP
@thread_safety This function must only be called from the main thread\&.
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBgamepad\fP 
.PP
\fBglfwUpdateGamepadMappings\fP 
.PP
\fBglfwJoystickIsGamepad\fP
.RE
.PP
\fBSince\fP
.RS 4
Added in version 3\&.3\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "\fBGLFWAPI\fP int glfwGetInputMode (\fBGLFWwindow\fP * window, int mode)"

.PP
Returns the value of an input option for the specified window\&. This function returns the value of an input option for the specified window\&. The mode must be one of \fBGLFW_CURSOR\fP, \fBGLFW_STICKY_KEYS\fP, \fBGLFW_STICKY_MOUSE_BUTTONS\fP, \fBGLFW_LOCK_KEY_MODS\fP or \fBGLFW_RAW_MOUSE_MOTION\fP\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIwindow\fP The window to query\&. 
.br
\fImode\fP One of \fRGLFW_CURSOR\fP, \fRGLFW_STICKY_KEYS\fP, \fRGLFW_STICKY_MOUSE_BUTTONS\fP, \fRGLFW_LOCK_KEY_MODS\fP or \fRGLFW_RAW_MOUSE_MOTION\fP\&.
.RE
.PP
@errors Possible errors include \fBGLFW_NOT_INITIALIZED\fP and \fBGLFW_INVALID_ENUM\fP\&.
.PP
@thread_safety This function must only be called from the main thread\&.
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBglfwSetInputMode\fP
.RE
.PP
\fBSince\fP
.RS 4
Added in version 3\&.0\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "\fBGLFWAPI\fP const float * glfwGetJoystickAxes (int jid, int * count)"

.PP
Returns the values of all axes of the specified joystick\&. This function returns the values of all axes of the specified joystick\&. Each element in the array is a value between -1\&.0 and 1\&.0\&.
.PP
If the specified joystick is not present this function will return \fRNULL\fP but will not generate an error\&. This can be used instead of first calling \fBglfwJoystickPresent\fP\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIjid\fP The \fBjoystick\fP to query\&. 
.br
\fIcount\fP Where to store the number of axis values in the returned array\&. This is set to zero if the joystick is not present or an error occurred\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
An array of axis values, or \fRNULL\fP if the joystick is not present or an \fBerror\fP occurred\&.
.RE
.PP
@errors Possible errors include \fBGLFW_NOT_INITIALIZED\fP, \fBGLFW_INVALID_ENUM\fP and \fBGLFW_PLATFORM_ERROR\fP\&.
.PP
@pointer_lifetime The returned array is allocated and freed by GLFW\&. You should not free it yourself\&. It is valid until the specified joystick is disconnected or the library is terminated\&.
.PP
@thread_safety This function must only be called from the main thread\&.
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBjoystick_axis\fP
.RE
.PP
\fBSince\fP
.RS 4
Added in version 3\&.0\&. Replaces \fRglfwGetJoystickPos\fP\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "\fBGLFWAPI\fP const unsigned char * glfwGetJoystickButtons (int jid, int * count)"

.PP
Returns the state of all buttons of the specified joystick\&. This function returns the state of all buttons of the specified joystick\&. Each element in the array is either \fRGLFW_PRESS\fP or \fRGLFW_RELEASE\fP\&.
.PP
For backward compatibility with earlier versions that did not have \fBglfwGetJoystickHats\fP, the button array also includes all hats, each represented as four buttons\&. The hats are in the same order as returned by \fBglfwGetJoystickHats\fP and are in the order \fIup\fP, \fIright\fP, \fIdown\fP and \fIleft\fP\&. To disable these extra buttons, set the \fBGLFW_JOYSTICK_HAT_BUTTONS\fP init hint before initialization\&.
.PP
If the specified joystick is not present this function will return \fRNULL\fP but will not generate an error\&. This can be used instead of first calling \fBglfwJoystickPresent\fP\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIjid\fP The \fBjoystick\fP to query\&. 
.br
\fIcount\fP Where to store the number of button states in the returned array\&. This is set to zero if the joystick is not present or an error occurred\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
An array of button states, or \fRNULL\fP if the joystick is not present or an \fBerror\fP occurred\&.
.RE
.PP
@errors Possible errors include \fBGLFW_NOT_INITIALIZED\fP, \fBGLFW_INVALID_ENUM\fP and \fBGLFW_PLATFORM_ERROR\fP\&.
.PP
@pointer_lifetime The returned array is allocated and freed by GLFW\&. You should not free it yourself\&. It is valid until the specified joystick is disconnected or the library is terminated\&.
.PP
@thread_safety This function must only be called from the main thread\&.
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBjoystick_button\fP
.RE
.PP
\fBSince\fP
.RS 4
Added in version 2\&.2\&. @glfw3 Changed to return a dynamic array\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "\fBGLFWAPI\fP const char * glfwGetJoystickGUID (int jid)"

.PP
Returns the SDL compatible GUID of the specified joystick\&. This function returns the SDL compatible GUID, as a UTF-8 encoded hexadecimal string, of the specified joystick\&. The returned string is allocated and freed by GLFW\&. You should not free it yourself\&.
.PP
The GUID is what connects a joystick to a gamepad mapping\&. A connected joystick will always have a GUID even if there is no gamepad mapping assigned to it\&.
.PP
If the specified joystick is not present this function will return \fRNULL\fP but will not generate an error\&. This can be used instead of first calling \fBglfwJoystickPresent\fP\&.
.PP
The GUID uses the format introduced in SDL 2\&.0\&.5\&. This GUID tries to uniquely identify the make and model of a joystick but does not identify a specific unit, e\&.g\&. all wired Xbox 360 controllers will have the same GUID on that platform\&. The GUID for a unit may vary between platforms depending on what hardware information the platform specific APIs provide\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIjid\fP The \fBjoystick\fP to query\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The UTF-8 encoded GUID of the joystick, or \fRNULL\fP if the joystick is not present or an \fBerror\fP occurred\&.
.RE
.PP
@errors Possible errors include \fBGLFW_NOT_INITIALIZED\fP, \fBGLFW_INVALID_ENUM\fP and \fBGLFW_PLATFORM_ERROR\fP\&.
.PP
@pointer_lifetime The returned string is allocated and freed by GLFW\&. You should not free it yourself\&. It is valid until the specified joystick is disconnected or the library is terminated\&.
.PP
@thread_safety This function must only be called from the main thread\&.
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBgamepad\fP
.RE
.PP
\fBSince\fP
.RS 4
Added in version 3\&.3\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "\fBGLFWAPI\fP const unsigned char * glfwGetJoystickHats (int jid, int * count)"

.PP
Returns the state of all hats of the specified joystick\&. This function returns the state of all hats of the specified joystick\&. Each element in the array is one of the following values:
.PP
Name   Value    \fRGLFW_HAT_CENTERED\fP   0    \fRGLFW_HAT_UP\fP   1    \fRGLFW_HAT_RIGHT\fP   2    \fRGLFW_HAT_DOWN\fP   4    \fRGLFW_HAT_LEFT\fP   8    \fRGLFW_HAT_RIGHT_UP\fP   \fRGLFW_HAT_RIGHT\fP | \fRGLFW_HAT_UP\fP    \fRGLFW_HAT_RIGHT_DOWN\fP   \fRGLFW_HAT_RIGHT\fP | \fRGLFW_HAT_DOWN\fP    \fRGLFW_HAT_LEFT_UP\fP   \fRGLFW_HAT_LEFT\fP | \fRGLFW_HAT_UP\fP    \fRGLFW_HAT_LEFT_DOWN\fP   \fRGLFW_HAT_LEFT\fP | \fRGLFW_HAT_DOWN\fP   
.PP
The diagonal directions are bitwise combinations of the primary (up, right, down and left) directions and you can test for these individually by ANDing it with the corresponding direction\&.
.PP
.PP
.nf
if (hats[2] & GLFW_HAT_RIGHT)
{
    // State of hat 2 could be right\-up, right or right\-down
}
.fi
.PP
.PP
If the specified joystick is not present this function will return \fRNULL\fP but will not generate an error\&. This can be used instead of first calling \fBglfwJoystickPresent\fP\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIjid\fP The \fBjoystick\fP to query\&. 
.br
\fIcount\fP Where to store the number of hat states in the returned array\&. This is set to zero if the joystick is not present or an error occurred\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
An array of hat states, or \fRNULL\fP if the joystick is not present or an \fBerror\fP occurred\&.
.RE
.PP
@errors Possible errors include \fBGLFW_NOT_INITIALIZED\fP, \fBGLFW_INVALID_ENUM\fP and \fBGLFW_PLATFORM_ERROR\fP\&.
.PP
@pointer_lifetime The returned array is allocated and freed by GLFW\&. You should not free it yourself\&. It is valid until the specified joystick is disconnected, this function is called again for that joystick or the library is terminated\&.
.PP
@thread_safety This function must only be called from the main thread\&.
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBjoystick_hat\fP
.RE
.PP
\fBSince\fP
.RS 4
Added in version 3\&.3\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "\fBGLFWAPI\fP const char * glfwGetJoystickName (int jid)"

.PP
Returns the name of the specified joystick\&. This function returns the name, encoded as UTF-8, of the specified joystick\&. The returned string is allocated and freed by GLFW\&. You should not free it yourself\&.
.PP
If the specified joystick is not present this function will return \fRNULL\fP but will not generate an error\&. This can be used instead of first calling \fBglfwJoystickPresent\fP\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIjid\fP The \fBjoystick\fP to query\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The UTF-8 encoded name of the joystick, or \fRNULL\fP if the joystick is not present or an \fBerror\fP occurred\&.
.RE
.PP
@errors Possible errors include \fBGLFW_NOT_INITIALIZED\fP, \fBGLFW_INVALID_ENUM\fP and \fBGLFW_PLATFORM_ERROR\fP\&.
.PP
@pointer_lifetime The returned string is allocated and freed by GLFW\&. You should not free it yourself\&. It is valid until the specified joystick is disconnected or the library is terminated\&.
.PP
@thread_safety This function must only be called from the main thread\&.
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBjoystick_name\fP
.RE
.PP
\fBSince\fP
.RS 4
Added in version 3\&.0\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "\fBGLFWAPI\fP \fBvoid\fP * glfwGetJoystickUserPointer (int jid)"

.PP
Returns the user pointer of the specified joystick\&. This function returns the current value of the user-defined pointer of the specified joystick\&. The initial value is \fRNULL\fP\&.
.PP
This function may be called from the joystick callback, even for a joystick that is being disconnected\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIjid\fP The joystick whose pointer to return\&.
.RE
.PP
@errors Possible errors include \fBGLFW_NOT_INITIALIZED\fP\&.
.PP
@thread_safety This function may be called from any thread\&. Access is not synchronized\&.
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBjoystick_userptr\fP 
.PP
\fBglfwSetJoystickUserPointer\fP
.RE
.PP
\fBSince\fP
.RS 4
Added in version 3\&.3\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "\fBGLFWAPI\fP int glfwGetKey (\fBGLFWwindow\fP * window, int key)"

.PP
Returns the last reported state of a keyboard key for the specified window\&. This function returns the last state reported for the specified key to the specified window\&. The returned state is one of \fRGLFW_PRESS\fP or \fRGLFW_RELEASE\fP\&. The action \fRGLFW_REPEAT\fP is only reported to the key callback\&.
.PP
If the \fBGLFW_STICKY_KEYS\fP input mode is enabled, this function returns \fRGLFW_PRESS\fP the first time you call it for a key that was pressed, even if that key has already been released\&.
.PP
The key functions deal with physical keys, with \fBkey tokens\fP named after their use on the standard US keyboard layout\&. If you want to input text, use the Unicode character callback instead\&.
.PP
The \fBmodifier key bit masks\fP are not key tokens and cannot be used with this function\&.
.PP
\fBDo not use this function\fP to implement \fBtext input\fP\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIwindow\fP The desired window\&. 
.br
\fIkey\fP The desired \fBkeyboard key\fP\&. \fRGLFW_KEY_UNKNOWN\fP is not a valid key for this function\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
One of \fRGLFW_PRESS\fP or \fRGLFW_RELEASE\fP\&.
.RE
.PP
@errors Possible errors include \fBGLFW_NOT_INITIALIZED\fP and \fBGLFW_INVALID_ENUM\fP\&.
.PP
@thread_safety This function must only be called from the main thread\&.
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBinput_key\fP
.RE
.PP
\fBSince\fP
.RS 4
Added in version 1\&.0\&. @glfw3 Added window handle parameter\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "\fBGLFWAPI\fP const char * glfwGetKeyName (int key, int scancode)"

.PP
Returns the layout-specific name of the specified printable key\&. This function returns the name of the specified printable key, encoded as UTF-8\&. This is typically the character that key would produce without any modifier keys, intended for displaying key bindings to the user\&. For dead keys, it is typically the diacritic it would add to a character\&.
.PP
\fBDo not use this function\fP for \fBtext input\fP\&. You will break text input for many languages even if it happens to work for yours\&.
.PP
If the key is \fRGLFW_KEY_UNKNOWN\fP, the scancode is used to identify the key, otherwise the scancode is ignored\&. If you specify a non-printable key, or \fRGLFW_KEY_UNKNOWN\fP and a scancode that maps to a non-printable key, this function returns \fRNULL\fP but does not emit an error\&.
.PP
This behavior allows you to always pass in the arguments in the \fBkey callback\fP without modification\&.
.PP
The printable keys are:
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRGLFW_KEY_APOSTROPHE\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRGLFW_KEY_COMMA\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRGLFW_KEY_MINUS\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRGLFW_KEY_PERIOD\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRGLFW_KEY_SLASH\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRGLFW_KEY_SEMICOLON\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRGLFW_KEY_EQUAL\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRGLFW_KEY_LEFT_BRACKET\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRGLFW_KEY_RIGHT_BRACKET\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRGLFW_KEY_BACKSLASH\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRGLFW_KEY_WORLD_1\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRGLFW_KEY_WORLD_2\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRGLFW_KEY_0\fP to \fRGLFW_KEY_9\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRGLFW_KEY_A\fP to \fRGLFW_KEY_Z\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRGLFW_KEY_KP_0\fP to \fRGLFW_KEY_KP_9\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRGLFW_KEY_KP_DECIMAL\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRGLFW_KEY_KP_DIVIDE\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRGLFW_KEY_KP_MULTIPLY\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRGLFW_KEY_KP_SUBTRACT\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRGLFW_KEY_KP_ADD\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRGLFW_KEY_KP_EQUAL\fP
.PP
.PP
Names for printable keys depend on keyboard layout, while names for non-printable keys are the same across layouts but depend on the application language and should be localized along with other user interface text\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIkey\fP The key to query, or \fRGLFW_KEY_UNKNOWN\fP\&. 
.br
\fIscancode\fP The scancode of the key to query\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The UTF-8 encoded, layout-specific name of the key, or \fRNULL\fP\&.
.RE
.PP
@errors Possible errors include \fBGLFW_NOT_INITIALIZED\fP, \fBGLFW_INVALID_VALUE\fP, \fBGLFW_INVALID_ENUM\fP and \fBGLFW_PLATFORM_ERROR\fP\&.
.PP
\fBRemarks\fP
.RS 4
The contents of the returned string may change when a keyboard layout change event is received\&.
.RE
.PP
@pointer_lifetime The returned string is allocated and freed by GLFW\&. You should not free it yourself\&. It is valid until the library is terminated\&.
.PP
@thread_safety This function must only be called from the main thread\&.
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBinput_key_name\fP
.RE
.PP
\fBSince\fP
.RS 4
Added in version 3\&.2\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "\fBGLFWAPI\fP int glfwGetKeyScancode (int key)"

.PP
Returns the platform-specific scancode of the specified key\&. This function returns the platform-specific scancode of the specified key\&.
.PP
If the specified \fBkey token\fP corresponds to a physical key not supported on the current platform then this method will return \fR-1\fP\&. Calling this function with anything other than a key token will return \fR-1\fP and generate a \fBGLFW_INVALID_ENUM\fP error\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIkey\fP Any \fBkey token\fP\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
The platform-specific scancode for the key, or \fR-1\fP if the key is not supported on the current platform or an \fBerror\fP occurred\&.
.RE
.PP
@errors Possible errors include \fBGLFW_NOT_INITIALIZED\fP and \fBGLFW_INVALID_ENUM\fP\&.
.PP
@thread_safety This function may be called from any thread\&.
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBinput_key\fP
.RE
